</div></section></section><section id="tbed14353-sec-0170"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">3.5. 
<em>E. coli</em> F18 outbreak</h3>
<p>On day 4 post‐challenge, the site manager reported signs of elevated mortality with cyanosis of extremities in rooms 1–3. Necropsy indicated lesions of haemorrhagic enteritis with gas in multiple pigs, suggestive of <em>E. coli</em> septicaemia (Figure <a href="#tbed14353-fig-0002" class="usa-link">2</a>). All animals in the study were immediately treated intramuscularly with enrofloxacin (Baytril, Elanco, Greenfield, IN) and orally via the drinking water with gentamycin<em>. E. coli</em> positive for F18 pillus and LT, STa, STb, Stx2 and Stx2e toxins were recovered from two sets of tissue samples sent to Iowa State University. No evidence of PEDv was observed in the tissues. Animals exhibited reduced growth and elevated mortality (Table <a href="#tbed14353-tbl-0006" class="usa-link">6</a>).</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="tbed14353-fig-0002"><h4 class="obj_head">FIGURE 2.</h4>
